    $astroMoon_master$> The Moon is not a planet, yet it belongs in this unit for three reasons. **It is anomalous:** at 27% of Earth's diameter and 1/81 of its mass, it is *fifty times larger* relative to its planet than any other major moon in the solar system. Earth does not have a moon in the ordinary sense; it has a **companion**, and that demands explaining. **It is our chronometer:** crater counting dates surfaces everywhere, but that method has exactly one absolute calibration — Apollo and Luna samples from sites whose crater densities we had measured — so *every age quoted anywhere in this block, for Mercury, Venus, or Mars, ultimately rests on lunar rocks.* **And it may be a precondition for us:** the Moon's steady torque appears to hold Earth's axial tilt steady against chaos, and Mars, which has no large moon, does swing chaotically — confirming the mechanism. The centrepiece is the origin: the **giant impact**, in which a Mars-sized body struck the proto-Earth and the Moon condensed from the debris. The evidence is strong and fivefold — yet it fails on the one measurement that should have been easiest. Simulations say the Moon should be built mostly from the impactor, and bodies from different parts of the disk carry distinct isotopic fingerprints; so the Moon should be isotopically distinguishable from Earth. **It is not.** By every isotopic test, the Moon looks as though it is made of Earth. That is the isotopic crisis, and it is the most interesting problem in Unit II.

## The mirrors that are still working

On 21 July 1969 Armstrong and Aldrin set out, among their instruments, a briefcase-sized panel of **corner-cube retroreflectors** — glass prisms that return any light striking them straight back the way it came, whatever the angle. Apollo 14 and 15 left larger arrays; the Soviet Lunokhod rovers carried French-built ones. Then everyone went home, and **those mirrors are still working.** Every clear night, observatories fire pulsed lasers at them and time the return. The beam spreads to kilometres across by the time it arrives, and only a handful of photons per pulse make it back — but the round-trip is timed to a precision of a few millimetres.

Half a century of that has produced one of the most precisely known quantities in planetary science, and it is not static: **the Moon is receding from Earth at 3.8 centimetres a year** — about the rate fingernails grow. That sounds trivial and is not, because it is the symptom of a deep process: angular momentum is being continuously transferred from Earth's spin to the Moon's orbit. The Moon climbs to a higher orbit, and it is paid for out of the length of our day, which is lengthening by about 1.8 milliseconds per century. And here is what lifts this above a curiosity — it can be checked against something utterly unrelated. If days have grown longer, the deep past held *more* of them per year, and certain fossil corals lay down daily growth bands grouped into annual patterns. Devonian corals, 400 million years old, record roughly **400 days per year** — a 22-hour day. **A laser bounced off a mirror on the Moon, and a 400-million-year-old coral, agree.**

{{image: Lunar Laser Ranging experiment | The retroreflector array Apollo 11 left on the Sea of Tranquility — a panel of corner-cube prisms with no power and no moving parts, which return a laser pulse straight back to its source. Half a century on, it and its siblings from Apollo 14 and 15 still work: observatories time the round trip of a laser pulse to a few millimetres, and the accumulated record shows the Moon receding at 3.8 cm per year as angular momentum drains from Earth's spin into the lunar orbit. It is one of the longest-running and most precise experiments in planetary science, and it needs nothing at the far end but a mirror left by astronauts.}}

## A moon that shouldn't be there

Before explaining the Moon's origin, be clear about what needs explaining. Two facts do the work. **First, it is far too large.** Moons are, as a rule, minute beside their planets: Titan is 1/4,000 of Saturn, Ganymede — the largest moon of all, bigger than Mercury — just 1/12,000 of Jupiter. Our Moon is 1/81 of Earth. That rules out the ordinary routes. The giants' regular moons formed from **circumplanetary disks**, miniature nebulae that make only small satellites; and **capture** produces distant, inclined, often retrograde orbits, like the giants' irregular moons. Our Moon is large, close, and in a regular prograde orbit near Earth's equatorial plane. It looks neither disk-born nor captured.

```example
moon-mass-ratio
```

**Second, it is made of the wrong stuff.** The Moon's mean density is **3,342 kg/m³** against Earth's 5,514 — and, being small, the Moon is barely compressed, so that number really is its composition. Apollo seismometers and later gravity mapping show why: its iron core is tiny, about 330 km in radius — some 19% of the Moon's radius and under 1% of its volume, against Earth's 55% and 16%. **The Moon is strongly depleted in iron.** Its bulk resembles not a whole planet but a planet's *mantle* — silicate rock with the metal removed. That is a remarkably specific clue: something made a body out of mantle material and left the iron behind.

```example
lunar-core-fraction
```

## Born in fire

The explanation that accounts for both anomalies became the consensus by the mid-1980s, and it fits Unit I exactly. Recall from L5 that the terrestrial planets finished assembling in a chaotic phase of **giant impacts**. One of the last of them, about 4.5 billion years ago, was a Mars-sized body — named **Theia** — striking the proto-Earth an **oblique**, moderate-velocity blow. Theia's iron core sank through and **merged with Earth's core**; a great quantity of silicate mantle from both bodies was flung into orbit, much of it vaporised; and that debris disk cooled, condensed, and **accreted into the Moon**, perhaps within a century. The glancing geometry spun the system up.

Five independent lines support this, and their convergence is what persuaded the field. **The iron depletion** falls out directly — the Moon is mantle material, the impactor's core kept by Earth. **The excess angular momentum** of the Earth–Moon system, far more than ordinary accretion yields, is what a glancing collision supplies. **The volatile depletion** — the Moon is poor in water, potassium, sodium, zinc — is exactly what vaporising and recondensing the material would do. (Note the contrast with L8: this same reasoning *undermined* the impact story for Mercury, where MESSENGER found volatile *enrichment*. Here it works.) **The anorthosite highlands** are a floated feldspar crust, which needs a global **magma ocean** — an origin hot enough that only an impact supplies it. And **the orbital geometry**, close to Earth's equatorial plane, is what a debris disk around a spinning Earth predicts. Five distinct observations, one mechanism it was not built to explain. And then there is a sixth measurement, which does not fit at all.

{{image: Giant-impact hypothesis | An artist's impression of the giant impact: a Mars-sized body, Theia, striking the proto-Earth an oblique blow around 4.5 billion years ago. The impactor's iron core sank and merged with Earth's, while silicate mantle from both worlds was thrown into orbit — vaporised, then recondensed and reaccreted into the Moon within perhaps a century. The scenario explains, in one stroke, the Moon's iron-poor composition, the excess angular momentum of the Earth–Moon system, its depleted volatiles, its magma-ocean crust, and its orbit. It also makes a clear prediction about what the Moon should be made of — and that is exactly where the trouble begins.}}

## The Moon is made of Earth

Here is the difficulty. The canonical simulations make a structural prediction: because the Moon forms largely from material thrown off the impactor, **it should be mostly Theia** — typically 70–90%. That is testable, because bodies that formed in different regions of the disk carry different isotopic compositions, especially in oxygen, where the ratios of ¹⁶O, ¹⁷O, and ¹⁸O vary with formation location and survive melting and differentiation unchanged. These ratios are a **fingerprint of where a body was built** — and we know the method works, because meteorites from Mars are isotopically distinct from Earth, those from Vesta distinct from both, and the chondrite groups occupy their own fields. So if the Moon is mostly Theia, and Theia formed elsewhere, the difference should be obvious.

```isotopecrisis
```

It is not merely small. **It is absent.** Lunar and terrestrial oxygen isotopes agree to within about **five parts per million**, the limit of measurement — and titanium, chromium, silicon, and tungsten match too. By every isotopic test available, the Moon is made of Earth. Sit with the structure of this: the giant impact explains five things it was not designed to explain, and fails on the one measurement that should have been the most straightforward confirmation of all. It is the same shape of problem we have met twice before — the **metre-size barrier** (L5) and **Mercury's volatiles** (L8) — a well-supported framework breaking on a single, sharply-posed quantity. The proposed escapes all try to erase the predicted fingerprint: Theia forming at Earth's own orbital radius and so sharing its composition; the hot debris disk **equilibrating** with Earth's vaporised outer layers before the Moon condensed; or a far more violent impact that vaporised *both* worlds into a single connected structure of rock vapour — a **synestia** — from which a thoroughly mixed Moon condensed. The impact framework is not in doubt; the specific scenario is being rewritten, and this measurement is what drives it.

## Two terrains, one clock

The Moon's face divides, visibly, into two. The bright, rugged **highlands** — anorthosite, the magma-ocean crust — cover 83% of the surface, date to about **4.4 billion years**, and are saturated with craters: essentially the original surface, unchanged. The dark, smooth **maria** ("seas") are flood basalts that filled great impact basins, cover 17%, date to **3.1–3.9 billion years**, and carry perhaps a tenth the crater density because they reset the count. That contrast is L7's chronology principle made visible to the naked eye: **crater density measures age.**

{{image: Moon | The full Moon, its two terrains plain even to the eye: the bright, ancient, heavily cratered highlands — anorthosite crust that floated on a global magma ocean 4.4 billion years ago — and the dark, smoother maria, basaltic lava that flooded great impact basins between 3.1 and 3.9 billion years ago and reset the crater count. Because Apollo and Luna returned rock from sites whose crater densities had been measured from orbit, the Moon carries the only absolute calibration of crater dating that exists — so every surface age in this block, for every world, ultimately traces back to these rocks. Note too how one-sided the maria are: they cover about 31% of the near side but only 2% of the far side, an asymmetry still not fully explained.}}

Now the point that justifies the lecture. Crater counting gives *relative* ages anywhere we have images, but converting them to *absolute* ages needs calibration — and that calibration exists in exactly one place. **Apollo and Luna returned samples from six sites whose crater densities were known**, and radiometric dating of those rocks fixes the curve relating crater density to time. Extended to every other inner-solar-system body, that one curve is why L8 could date Mercury's surface, L9 could date Venus's resurfacing, and L12 will date the Martian valley networks. **The Moon is the only absolutely-calibrated clock we possess, and it dates the whole neighbourhood** — which is also why its limits matter (six near-side sites, several perhaps dominated by Imbrium ejecta) and why China's 2024 Chang'e 6 far-side return is a real advance. And the Moon is dead exactly as L7 predicts: with a surface-to-volume ratio **3.67× Earth's** it cooled fast; volcanism ceased ~1 Gyr ago; escape velocity 2.38 km/s holds no atmosphere; and paleomagnetism in Apollo samples shows a core dynamo that ran from 4.2 to 3.5 Gyr and then stopped — the same size-driven sequence we will see on Mars, run earlier and to completion. **Its deadness is what makes it useful.**

## The Moon is leaving

Return to the cold open and work out the mechanism. The Moon raises tidal bulges in Earth's oceans and solid body. If Earth did not rotate, they would point straight at the Moon — but Earth turns once a day while the Moon orbits once in 27.3 days, so friction drags the bulges **ahead** of the Earth–Moon line. By Newton's third law the leading bulge pulls the Moon **forward**, adding angular momentum so it spirals **outward**, while the Moon pulls **back** on the bulge, braking Earth's spin. Momentum moves from Earth's rotation to the lunar orbit; friction dissipates the difference as heat.

```tidalrecession
```

The same physics, run earlier and harder on the smaller body, is why the Moon keeps one face toward us: Earth's tides dissipated the Moon's spin until it matched its orbit — **tidal locking**, the 1:1 resonance we contrasted with Mercury's 3:2. And running the clock back, the Moon was once far closer — tides falling as 1/r³, so at half the distance they were eightfold stronger — with a shorter month, a faster-spinning Earth, and a Moon looming huge in the sky.

```example
tidal-day-length
```

But note a caution worth generalising. Naively running 3.8 cm/yr backwards puts the Moon at Earth's surface 1.5 billion years ago — certainly wrong, since the Moon is 4.5 billion years old. The error is assuming a *constant* rate: tidal dissipation depends strongly on the shape of ocean basins, which plate tectonics (L10) keeps rearranging, so the present rate is anomalously high and past rates were mostly lower. Better models place the Moon's formation at **3–5 Earth radii** — very close, consistent with the impact. **Extrapolating a measured rate far beyond its interval is hazardous whenever the rate depends on conditions that themselves change.**

## The stabilizer

Now the claim that connects the Moon to habitability, stated carefully because it is both important and contested. Earth's axial tilt is 23.4°, and — this is what matters — it is remarkably **steady**, varying only between about 22.1° and 24.5° on a regular 41,000-year cycle. Simulations of a **moonless** Earth (Laskar and colleagues, early 1990s) find something very different: the obliquity would vary **chaotically**, from near 0° to as much as 85°, over millions of years. The mechanism is a resonance — a planet's spin axis precesses at a rate set by the torques on its bulge, and if that rate falls near one of the frequencies at which the other planets perturb its orbit, the tilt goes chaotic. The Moon adds a large, steady torque that keeps Earth's precession **fast and regular**, safely away from those resonances. It is a gyroscopic stabiliser.

That would be an untestable simulation but for one case next door. **Mars has no large moon** (Phobos and Deimos are captured asteroids of negligible mass), and its obliquity is known to vary chaotically between roughly 0° and more than 60° — driving dramatic climate swings that redistribute its ice, a story L12 takes up. **The prediction is confirmed on the one available test.** The honest caveat: the effect is real, but its *severity* for habitability is debated — later work suggests the chaos might be milder than first thought, and even large swings would make a harsher climate, not a sterile world. So the defensible statement is precise: the Moon measurably steadies Earth's tilt; without it our climate history would have been far more variable; Mars shows the mechanism operates; and whether this rises to a *precondition* for complex life is not established. That is a good model for holding a claim — **a well-established effect, an empirical confirmation, and an honest uncertainty about its ultimate weight.**

## The companion

Gather the argument. We began with **mirrors that still work**, revealing a Moon that recedes 3.8 cm/yr while our day lengthens — confirmed with unusual elegance by 400-million-year-old corals recording ~400 days a year. The Moon is **anomalous**: fifty times too large relative to its planet for a disk or a capture to explain, and iron-poor, built of mantle rather than whole-planet material. The **giant impact** accounts for both and for three things more — the angular momentum, the volatile depletion, the magma-ocean crust — five clues, one collision. And then the **isotopic crisis**: the Moon should be mostly Theia and so isotopically distinct, yet it matches Earth to five parts per million, the third time in this block a good theory has broken on the easiest measurement. The surface gives us the solar system's **only calibrated clock**, so every date in Unit II rests on lunar rock; and the Moon is dead precisely as its size predicts. **Tidal evolution** drains momentum from our spin into its orbit and once locked its face toward us; and the Moon may be quietly **steadying our tilt**, with Mars as the confirming counter-example.

Here is what to take from it. The Moon is not an ornament. It is the **debris of a planetary collision** — a body made from Earth's own mantle by an event violent enough to melt a world — and it has shaped our planet ever since, braking our rotation, raising our tides, steadying our axis. It is also, and this is easy to miss, **the instrument that makes the rest of this block quantitative**: every age for every world rests on rocks that twelve people carried home from six sites on one hemisphere of one satellite. A single sample-return campaign, half a century old, calibrates the chronology of the inner solar system — and its value has *grown*, as those samples are re-analysed with instruments that did not exist when they were collected. **A returned sample is not a measurement but a permanent capability to make future measurements**, which is the strongest argument there is for bringing material home. **Next: Mars, and the close of Unit II — the world that had running water, a thicker atmosphere, and a magnetic field, and lost all three, where the chain from a planet's *size* to its *habitability* finally runs to completion.**$astroMoon_master$,
    true,
    true
